            <ArticleTitle>Chronic (neuropathic) corneal pain and blepharospasm: five case reports.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2427-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pain.2011.06.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pain and focal dystonias have been associated with chronic pain conditions such as complex regional pain syndrome. Corneal pain, frequently known as &quot;dry eye&quot;, may be a neuropathic pain condition with abnormalities of the nerve plexus. Here we present 5 case histories of patients with defined corneal pain (with associated neuropathic features) and objective measures of changes in the nerve plexus and associated blepharospasm. A putative relationship between pain and blepharospasm suggests potential involvement of the basal ganglia in both these conditions.</AbstractText>
